\id 1CO - Restored Context Bible (RCB)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h 1 Corinthians
\toc1 The First Letter of Paul to the Assembly in Corinth
\toc2 1 Corinthians
\toc3 1Co
\mt1 1 Corinthians

\imt Introduction
\ip This letter is from Paul, writing from Ephesus somewhere around the middle of the first century, to the assembly he had founded in Corinth. He had come to the city alone and unimpressive, worked with his hands as a tentmaker, and stayed about eighteen months. The community that resulted was his own work, and he never speaks to it as a stranger.
\ip Corinth was a Roman colony, rebuilt on the isthmus a century after Rome destroyed the old Greek city, and by Paul's day it was the capital of the province of Achaia and rich on the trade that crossed it. It was crowded with temples and their dining rooms, and it was fiercely admiring of skilled speakers. A man's worth was measured there by how he performed and whom he could claim as a patron. Almost every problem in this letter is that measurement carried indoors and applied to the assembly of God.
\ip Paul writes because two things reached him. Members of Chloe's household arrived with news that the community had split into parties around its teachers, that a man was living with his father's wife and no one had acted, and that the common meal had become an occasion for the rich to eat in front of the hungry. And a letter came from the Corinthians themselves, carried by Stephanas, Fortunatus and Achaicus, with a list of questions: about marriage and celibacy, about meat that had been through a temple, about spiritual gifts, about the relief fund for Jerusalem. Paul answers the reports first and then works through their questions one by one, marking each with "Now concerning." This is not his first letter to them; an earlier one is mentioned in passing and has not survived.
\ip Underneath every answer runs a single argument. A crucified Messiah overturns the whole scale by which Corinth ranks people, and a community founded on him cannot go on ranking itself by the scale he overturned. That is why the letter moves so easily from the cross to table manners: the same claim governs both. It reaches its foundation only at the end, in the long argument that Christ was raised bodily and that those who belong to him will be raised too — which is why nothing done in his service is wasted.

\c 1
\s1 Greeting
\p
\v 1 Paul, called to be one sent\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Greek \tl apostolos\tl*: an emissary who carries the full authority of the one who sent him. Rendered "one sent" throughout.\f* of Christ Jesus through the will of God \add [— no human council put me here]\add*, and Sosthenes the brother\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Very likely the synagogue ruler in Corinth who was beaten in front of Gallio's tribunal (Acts 18:17). The Corinthians would have known the name and what had become of the man.\f*,
\v 2 to the assembly\f + \fr 1:2 \ft Greek \tl ekklēsia\tl*, the word the Greek scriptures use for the assembly of Israel gathered before God (Deuteronomy 4:10; 1 Kings 8:14). Rendered "assembly" throughout.\f* of God that is in Corinth \add [— it is God's, not the city's and not mine]\add*, to those made holy in Christ Jesus, called to be holy ones\f + \fr 1:2 \ft Greek \tl hagioi\tl*, "set-apart ones" — Israel's own covenant word for itself (Exodus 19:6; Deuteronomy 7:6). It says whose they are, not how they are behaving.\f*, together with all who in every place call on the name of our Lord Jesus Christ\f + \fr 1:2 \ft "Everyone who calls on the name of the LORD will be rescued" (Joel 2:32). The name Joel means is the divine name.\f* — their Lord and ours:
\v 3 Favor\f + \fr 1:3 \ft Greek \tl charis\tl*, rendered "favor" throughout: God's freely given goodwill and the concrete generosity it produces. The same word will name the relief fund for Jerusalem at 16:3.\f* to you and peace from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ.
\s1 I Thank God for What He Has Given You
\p
\v 4 I give thanks to my God always concerning you, for the favor of God given to you in Christ Jesus,
\v 5 that in everything you were made rich in him, in every word and in all knowledge \add [— speech and understanding, both of them handed to you]\add*,
\v 6 just as the testimony of the Christ was made firm among you\f + \fr 1:6 \ft Greek \tl bebaioō\tl*, the word for the guarantee a seller gave that a sale would stand. The same verb returns in verse 8: the God who guaranteed the message will guarantee you.\f*,
\v 7 so that you come short in no gift while you wait eagerly for the uncovering\f + \fr 1:7 \ft Greek \tl apokalypsis\tl*, an unveiling — the moment when what is now hidden is brought out where it can be seen.\f* of our Lord Jesus Christ,
\v 8 who will also make you firm to the end, with no charge against you \add [i.e. with nothing left standing against you in court]\add* in the day of our Lord Jesus Christ\f + \fr 1:8 \ft The prophets' "day of the LORD," the day of final reckoning (Amos 5:18; Joel 2:1), here named as the day of Jesus.\f*.
\v 9 God is faithful, through whom you were called into a sharing in his Son, Jesus Christ our Lord.
\s1 The Tearing Apart of the Assembly
\p
\v 10 Now I call on you, brothers, through the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, that you all say the same thing, and that there be no tears among you, but that you be mended together\f + \fr 1:10 \ft Greek \tl schismata\tl*, tears — the word for a garment split along its seam (Matthew 9:16). "Mended together" is \tl katartizō\tl*, the word for repairing a fishing net (Mark 1:19). The picture is repair work, not theory.\f* in the same mind and in the same judgment.
\v 11 For it has been made clear to me about you, my brothers, by Chloe's people \add [i.e. by members of her household, who travel on her business]\add*, that there are quarrels among you.
\v 12 I mean this, that each of you is saying, "I am of Paul \add [— I belong to Paul]\add*," "I of Apollos\f + \fr 1:12 \ft An eloquent Alexandrian, powerful with the scriptures, who came to Corinth after Paul left (Acts 18:24-28). In a city that graded men by their speaking, he was magnetic.\f*," "I of Cephas\f + \fr 1:12 \ft The Aramaic name of Peter. Whether he ever came to Corinth or not, his name carried the prestige of Jerusalem.\f*," "I of Christ."
\v 13 Has the Christ been cut into parts \add [— one piece for each party?]\add*? Was Paul crucified for you? Or were you immersed\f + \fr 1:13 \ft Greek \tl baptizō\tl*, to dip or plunge; rendered "immerse" throughout. To be immersed "into a name" is to be handed over into that person's ownership and authority.\f* into the name of Paul?
\v 14 I give thanks that I immersed none of you except Crispus\f + \fr 1:14 \ft The ruler of the synagogue who came to faith with his whole household (Acts 18:8).\f* and Gaius,
\v 15 so that no one can say you were immersed into my name.
\v 16 I did immerse the household of Stephanas\f + \fr 1:16 \ft He and his household reappear at the end of the letter as the first converts in Achaia and its most tireless servants (16:15-18).\f* as well; beyond that I do not know whether I immersed anyone else.
\v 17 For Christ did not send me to immerse but to announce the good news — not with wisdom of speech \add [i.e. not dressed up in the polish this city pays to hear]\add*, so that the cross of the Christ would not be emptied out \add [i.e. drained of the very power that is in it]\add*.
\s1 The Word of the Cross
\p
\v 18 For the word of the cross\f + \fr 1:18 \ft Crucifixion was Rome's most degrading execution, reserved for slaves and rebels and forbidden to citizens. Cicero called it the cruelest and most revolting of penalties and said the word itself should be kept away from a Roman's ears. To announce a crucified Messiah was to announce the most shameful thing imaginable.\f* is foolishness to those who are perishing, but to us who are being rescued \add [— both of them still under way, neither finished]\add* it is the power of God.
\v 19 For it is written: "I will destroy the wisdom of the wise, and the understanding of the understanding I will set aside."\f + \fr 1:19 \ft Isaiah 29:14. In Isaiah, God says this against a people whose worship had become a performance learned by rote; his answer was to act in a way that shattered their whole framework.\f*
\v 20 Where is the wise man? Where is the scribe? Where is the debater of this age\f + \fr 1:20 \ft The taunt is the prophets' own — "Where are your wise men?" (Isaiah 19:12; 33:18) — thrown at powers that had looked unanswerable and were swept away. "This age" is the present order, over against the age to come.\f*? Has not God made the wisdom of the world foolish?
\v 21 For since, in the wisdom of God, the world did not know God through wisdom \add [— God saw to it that the road of clever reasoning would not arrive]\add*, God was pleased through the foolishness of what is proclaimed to rescue those who trust.
\v 22 Since Jews ask for signs \add [i.e. for the miraculous proof a Messiah was expected to give]\add* and Greeks look for wisdom \add [i.e. for an argument that satisfies the schools]\add*,
\v 23 but we proclaim Christ crucified — to Jews a stumbling block\f + \fr 1:23 \ft Greek \tl skandalon\tl*, the stick that springs a trap: not a disappointment but a thing that trips a man up. "Anyone hanged on a tree is under God's curse" (Deuteronomy 21:23), so the cross did not merely fail to prove the claim — it looked like proof against it.\f*, to the nations foolishness,
\v 24 but to those who are called, both Jews and Greeks, Christ the power of God and the wisdom of God \add [— the two things the cross appears to lack are the two things it is]\add*.
\v 25 Because the foolish thing of God is wiser than men, and the weak thing of God is stronger than men.
\s1 Look at Who God Called
\p
\v 26 For look at your calling, brothers: not many wise according to flesh, not many powerful, not many well-born \add [i.e. not many out of the families that run this city]\add*.
\v 27 But God chose out\f + \fr 1:27 \ft Greek \tl eklegomai\tl*, the Greek scriptures' word for God's choosing of Israel: "not because you were more numerous than any other people, for you were the fewest of all" (Deuteronomy 7:7). He has always chosen this way.\f* the foolish things of the world to shame the wise, and God chose out the weak things of the world to shame the strong,
\v 28 and God chose out the low-born things of the world and the things counted as nothing, the things that are not \add [i.e. what the world's ledger does not even record as existing]\add*, to bring to nothing the things that are,
\v 29 so that no flesh may boast before God.
\v 30 But out of him you are in Christ Jesus, who became wisdom to us from God — righteousness and holiness and ransoming\f + \fr 1:30 \ft Greek \tl apolytrōsis\tl*, buying back what has been lost or enslaved. It is the exodus word: God "ransomed" Israel out of Egypt (Exodus 6:6; Deuteronomy 7:8), and Israel called him her Redeemer.\f* —
\v 31 so that, as it is written, "Let the one who boasts, boast in the Lord."\f + \fr 1:31 \ft Jeremiah 9:23-24: "Let not the wise boast in his wisdom, nor the strong in his strength, nor the rich in his riches — but let him who boasts boast in this, that he knows me." Jeremiah names the same three things Paul has just said the Corinthians largely lack.\f*

\c 2
\s1 How I Came to You
\p
\v 1 And I, when I came to you, brothers, did not come with any high style of speech or of wisdom, announcing to you the secret of God.
\v 2 For I decided to know nothing among you except Jesus Christ, and him crucified \add [— I ruled out every other subject on purpose]\add*.
\v 3 And I was with you in weakness and in fear and in much trembling\f + \fr 2:3 \ft Not a figure of speech. He arrived in Corinth alone, out of a poor reception at Athens, without money and without the standing the city respected, and supported himself with his hands (Acts 18:1-5).\f*,
\v 4 and my word and my proclamation were not in persuasive words of wisdom but in a showing of Spirit and of power,
\v 5 so that your faithfulness might not rest in the wisdom of men but in the power of God \add [— a faith propped on a good speaker falls over when a better one arrives]\add*.
\s1 God's Wisdom, Hidden Before the Ages
\p
\v 6 Yet we do speak wisdom among the full-grown\f + \fr 2:6 \ft Greek \tl teleioi\tl*, those who have come to full growth — not an inner circle, but believers who have grown past infancy. The opposite word, "infants," lands on the Corinthians at 3:1.\f* — but not a wisdom of this age, nor of the rulers of this age\f + \fr 2:6 \ft The authorities who condemned and executed Jesus — the council and the governor — named as what they were: the men who held power in the present order. Compare Acts 3:17; 13:27.\f*, who are being brought to nothing.
\v 7 No, we speak God's wisdom in a secret\f + \fr 2:7 \ft Greek \tl mystērion\tl*, not a puzzle but a thing God kept hidden and has now brought out into the open. Rendered "secret" throughout.\f*, the wisdom that has been hidden away, which God marked out beforehand, before the ages, for our glory —
\v 8 which none of the rulers of this age has known; for if they had known it, they would not have crucified the Lord of glory \add [— the very act by which they showed their power was the act that undid them]\add*.
\v 9 But as it is written: "What eye has not seen and ear has not heard, and what has not come up upon the heart of man — these things God prepared for those who love him."\f + \fr 2:9 \ft Drawing on Isaiah 64:4. Eye, ear and heart are the whole of a man's means of finding anything out; the point is that all three fall short of this.\f*
\s1 The Spirit Searches the Deep Things of God
\p
\v 10 But to us God uncovered them through the Spirit; for the Spirit searches all things, even the deep things of God.
\v 11 For who among men knows the things of a man except the spirit of the man that is in him? So too no one has known the things of God except the Spirit of God \add [— as your own spirit is not a visitor reporting on you from outside, so the Spirit is God's own inwardness]\add*.
\v 12 And we did not receive the spirit of the world but the Spirit that is out of God, so that we might know the things freely given to us by God \add [i.e. so that we can recognize what is already in our hands]\add*.
\v 13 And these things we speak, not in words taught by human wisdom but in words taught by the Spirit, fitting spiritual things to what is spiritual.
\s1 Two Kinds of Men
\p
\v 14 But a creaturely man\f + \fr 2:14 \ft Greek \tl psychikos\tl*, from \tl psychē\tl*, the breath of natural life — the Hebrew \tl nephesh\tl*. It means a man running on his own faculties alone. He may be clever, cultured and decent; he simply has nothing in him that can pick this up. The same word describes the present body at 15:44.\f* does not take in the things of the Spirit of God, for they are foolishness to him, and he cannot know them, because they are examined spiritually \add [— the faculty does not match the object, as an ear cannot judge a color]\add*.
\v 15 The spiritual man examines all things, but he himself is examined by no one \add [i.e. no one working from natural faculties alone is equipped to weigh him]\add*.
\v 16 For "who has known the mind of the Lord, who will instruct him?"\f + \fr 2:16 \ft Isaiah 40:13, where the answer is: no one, ever. Paul quotes it and then says the impossible thing has been given away.\f* But we have the mind of Christ.

\c 3
\s1 You Are Still Infants
\p
\v 1 And I, brothers, could not speak to you as to spiritual men but as to men of flesh, as to infants in Christ \add [— in Christ, genuinely; but infants]\add*.
\v 2 I gave you milk to drink, not solid food, for you were not yet able. And even now you are still not able,
\v 3 for you are still fleshly. For where there is jealousy and quarreling among you, are you not fleshly, and walking the way men walk?
\v 4 For when one says, "I am of Paul," and another, "I of Apollos," are you not merely men \add [i.e. men grading teachers exactly as this city grades its speakers]\add*?
\s1 Servants in God's Field
\p
\v 5 What then is Apollos? And what is Paul \add [— not "who," but "what": ask what we are for]\add*? Servants through whom you came to trust, and to each as the Lord gave.
\v 6 I planted, Apollos watered, but God was making it grow \add [— we each did one thing once; he has not stopped for a moment]\add*.
\v 7 So neither the one who plants is anything nor the one who waters, but God who makes it grow.
\v 8 The one who plants and the one who waters are one, and each will receive his own wages according to his own labor \add [— one work, but each man answerable for his own share of it]\add*.
\v 9 For we are God's fellow workers; you are God's field\f + \fr 3:9 \ft Israel is God's planting throughout the scriptures — the vine brought out of Egypt (Psalm 80:8), the vineyard of the LORD of hosts (Isaiah 5:7). The field belongs to the owner, never to the hired hands.\f*, God's building.
\s1 Building on the Foundation
\p
\v 10 According to the favor of God given to me, like a wise master builder I laid a foundation, and another is building on it. But let each man watch how he builds on it.
\v 11 For no one can lay another foundation than the one already lying there, which is Jesus Christ \add [— that is settled; the only open question is what goes up on top]\add*.
\v 12 And if anyone builds on the foundation with gold, silver, precious stones, wood, hay, straw\f + \fr 3:12 \ft The first three are the materials of the temple (1 Chronicles 29:2); the last three are field cuttings and shelter thatch. The contrast is not good against evil but what survives fire against what does not.\f* —
\v 13 each man's work will become plain, for the Day will make it clear, because it is uncovered in fire; and the fire itself will test what sort of work each man's is\f + \fr 3:13 \ft In the prophets, fire does not destroy indiscriminately; it separates. "He is like a refiner's fire... he will refine them like gold and silver" (Malachi 3:2-3; compare Zechariah 13:9).\f*.
\v 14 If anyone's work that he built on it remains, he will receive wages.
\v 15 If anyone's work is burned up, he will suffer loss; but he himself will be rescued, yet so as through fire \add [i.e. like a man who gets out of a burning house alive and with nothing]\add*.
\s1 You Are God's Sanctuary
\p
\v 16 Do you not know\f + \fr 3:16 \ft "Do you not know?" runs ten times through this letter. Every time, it introduces something they already possess and are failing to act on.\f* that you are God's sanctuary\f + \fr 3:16 \ft Greek \tl naos\tl*, not the whole temple complex but the inner shrine where the God himself dwells. The "you" is plural: the community together is the place where God lives.\f* and that the Spirit of God dwells in you?
\v 17 If anyone ruins God's sanctuary, God will ruin him \add [— the sentence is the crime, said back]\add*; for God's sanctuary is holy, and that is what you are.
\s1 All Things Are Yours
\p
\v 18 Let no one deceive himself. If anyone among you thinks he is wise in this age, let him become foolish, so that he may become wise.
\v 19 For the wisdom of this world is foolishness with God. For it is written: "He catches the wise in their own craftiness,"\f + \fr 3:19 \ft Job 5:13. God does not merely out-think the cunning; he lets their own scheming become the trap that takes them — which is what happened at the cross.\f*
\v 20 and again: "The Lord knows the reasonings of the wise, that they are empty."\f + \fr 3:20 \ft Psalm 94:11, where the psalm says "of men"; the point is applied here to the wise in particular.\f*
\v 21 So let no one boast in men; for all things are yours —
\v 22 whether Paul or Apollos or Cephas, whether world or life or death, whether things present or things coming — all are yours \add [— you have been fighting over which servant you prefer, and the whole estate is already yours]\add*,
\v 23 and you are Christ's, and Christ is God's.

\c 4
\s1 Reckon Us as Household Managers
\p
\v 1 Let a man reckon us like this: as attendants\f + \fr 4:1 \ft Greek \tl hypēretēs\tl*, originally an under-rower on the lower bench of a galley: a man who pulls to another man's orders.\f* of Christ and household managers\f + \fr 4:1 \ft Greek \tl oikonomos\tl*, the slave or freedman who ran an estate. Everything he handed out belonged to someone else, and he answered to the owner for all of it.\f* of God's secrets.
\v 2 Now what is looked for in household managers is that a man be found faithful \add [— not impressive; faithful]\add*.
\v 3 But it is a very small thing to me that I should be examined by you, or by any human day \add [i.e. by any court that sits on any day men appoint]\add*. In fact I do not even examine myself.
\v 4 For I know nothing against myself, but I am not set right by that \add [— a clear conscience is not an acquittal]\add*; the one who examines me is the Lord.
\v 5 So do not judge anything before the time, until the Lord comes, who will bring to light the hidden things of darkness and make plain the counsels of hearts \add [i.e. what each man was actually trying to do, and why]\add*. And then the praise will come to each man from God.
\s1 What Do You Have That You Did Not Receive?
\p
\v 6 These things, brothers, I have applied to myself and Apollos for your sake \add [— we are the safe example; the quarrel is not really about us]\add*, so that through us you may learn the "nothing beyond what is written,"\f + \fr 4:6 \ft The scriptures he has been quoting since 1:19 all teach the one lesson: God overturns human wisdom, and the only boasting left is boasting in the Lord. To go "beyond what is written" is to set up rankings scripture has already forbidden.\f* so that none of you may be puffed up\f + \fr 4:6 \ft Greek \tl physioō\tl*, to inflate like a bladder. It appears seven times in this letter, more than in all Paul's other letters together, and it is his standing diagnosis of what is wrong in Corinth.\f* for the one against the other.
\v 7 For who marks you out as different? And what do you have that you did not receive? And if you did receive it, why do you boast as though you had not \add [— boasting about a gift denies that it was one]\add*?
\v 8 Already you are full! Already you have grown rich! Without us you have begun to reign! And how I wish you did reign, so that we also might reign with you \add [— if the kingdom had really arrived, we would not be living like this]\add*.
\s1 Last in the Procession
\p
\v 9 For it seems to me that God has put us, those sent, on show last of all, like men condemned to death\f + \fr 4:9 \ft The picture is a Roman triumph: the victorious general at the head, and at the very tail of the column the prisoners being marched to their execution in the arena. That is where Paul says God has placed the apostles.\f*, because we have become a spectacle to the world, both to angels and to men.
\v 10 We are fools for Christ's sake, but you are prudent in Christ. We are weak, but you are strong. You are held in honor, but we are without honor.
\v 11 Up to this present hour we go hungry and thirsty and poorly clothed, we are beaten with fists, we have no home,
\v 12 and we labor, working with our own hands\f + \fr 4:12 \ft Manual labor was despised by the educated classes; a teacher who supported himself rather than living from a patron was marking himself as a man of no standing. Paul chose it (9:15-18).\f*. Reviled, we bless; hunted down, we bear it;
\v 13 slandered, we speak kindly. We have become like the scourings of the world, the scrapings of everything\f + \fr 4:13 \ft Both words describe what is swept out after a cleaning. They also carried the memory of the human scapegoat driven out of a Greek city in time of plague, to take its pollution away with him.\f*, to this day.
\s1 I Am Your Father
\p
\v 14 I am not writing these things to shame you, but to put you in mind as my beloved children.
\v 15 For though you have ten thousand guardians\f + \fr 4:15 \ft Greek \tl paidagōgos\tl*, the household slave who walked a boy to school and kept him in order. Useful, and temporary. A boy might have many; he has one father.\f* in Christ, you do not have many fathers; for in Christ Jesus I fathered you through the good news.
\v 16 So I call on you: become imitators of me \add [— of the pattern I have just described, not of my manner of speaking]\add*.
\v 17 For this reason I sent you Timothy, who is my beloved and faithful child in the Lord, who will remind you of my ways in Christ Jesus, just as I teach everywhere in every assembly \add [— you are not a special case with rules of your own]\add*.
\s1 I Am Coming to You
\p
\v 18 Some have become puffed up, as though I were not coming to you \add [— they take Timothy's arrival to mean mine is off]\add*.
\v 19 But I will come to you soon, if the Lord wills, and I will find out not the talk of these puffed-up men but their power.
\v 20 For the kingdom of God is not in talk but in power.
\v 21 What do you want? Am I to come to you with a rod\f + \fr 4:21 \ft The rod is a father's instrument in the proverbs (Proverbs 13:24; 22:15), not a magistrate's. He has just called them his children.\f*, or in love and a spirit of gentleness?

\c 5
\s1 A Man Has His Father's Wife
\p
\v 1 It is actually reported that there is sexual immorality among you, and such immorality as is not even among the nations \add [i.e. that even pagan Corinth will not stomach]\add*: that a man has his father's wife\f + \fr 5:1 \ft Almost certainly a stepmother. The Torah forbids it flatly — "you shall not uncover the nakedness of your father's wife" (Leviticus 18:8; Deuteronomy 22:30) — and puts it under the covenant curse (Deuteronomy 27:20). Roman law forbade such a marriage as well.\f*.
\v 2 And you are puffed up! Should you not rather have mourned \add [— this calls for the grief you give a death]\add*, so that the one who did this deed might be taken out of your midst?
\v 3 For I, absent in body but present in spirit, have already judged the man who did this, as though I were present:
\v 4 in the name of our Lord Jesus, when you are gathered together and my spirit with you, with the power of our Lord Jesus,
\v 5 hand such a man over to Satan\f + \fr 5:5 \ft Not abandonment to a power outside God's reach. In Job 1-2 the adversary acts only by God's permission and within limits God sets. The man is put outside the assembly's protection, into the open country where the adversary works.\f* for the ruin of the flesh, so that the spirit may be rescued in the day of the Lord \add [— the whole severity is aimed at getting him there]\add*.
\s1 Clean Out the Old Leaven
\p
\v 6 Your boasting is not good. Do you not know that a little leaven leavens the whole lump \add [— it cannot be kept in the corner where you left it]\add*?
\v 7 Clean out the old leaven, so that you may be a new lump, since you are unleavened \add [— become what you already are]\add*. For our Passover lamb, Christ, has been sacrificed\f + \fr 5:7 \ft The searching-out of every crumb of leaven came before the lamb was killed (Exodus 12:15, 19). Paul's point is one of sequence: the lamb has already been slaughtered, so the leaven should long since have been gone. They are keeping the feast with the house unswept.\f*.
\v 8 So let us keep the feast \add [— our whole life now is the festival]\add*, not with old leaven, nor with the leaven of malice and wickedness, but with unleavened bread of sincerity and truth.
\s1 Whom You Are to Judge
\p
\v 9 I wrote to you in my letter\f + \fr 5:9 \ft An earlier letter to Corinth, which has not survived. They had read its instruction as a command to withdraw from the world.\f* not to mix with sexually immoral people —
\v 10 not meaning the sexually immoral of this world at large, or the greedy and the swindlers, or idolaters, since then you would have to go out of the world \add [— which is not what I meant and not what you can do]\add*.
\v 11 But now I write to you not to mix with anyone called a brother who is sexually immoral or greedy or an idolater or a reviler or a drunkard or a swindler — with such a man, not even to eat\f + \fr 5:11 \ft Sharing a table was the plainest public statement of fellowship there was. To refuse it was to say out loud that the name "brother" and the man's life no longer match.\f*.
\v 12 For what business is it of mine to judge those outside? Is it not those inside whom you judge?
\v 13 God will judge those outside. "Put away the evil one from among yourselves."\f + \fr 5:13 \ft The refrain of Deuteronomy's law code, repeated case after case (Deuteronomy 17:7; 19:19; 21:21; 22:21; 24:7). Paul closes the matter with Israel's own formula, applied to this assembly.\f*

\c 6
\s1 Taking a Brother to Court
\p
\v 1 Does any of you, having a matter against another, dare to go to law before the unrighteous\f + \fr 6:1 \ft The pagan magistrates. He does not call them judges or outsiders but "the unrighteous" — and the same word opens the list at verse 9 of those who will not inherit the kingdom.\f* and not before the holy ones?
\v 2 Or do you not know that the holy ones will judge the world? And if the world is judged by you, are you unworthy of the smallest cases\f + \fr 6:2 \ft "Judgment was given to the holy ones of the Most High" (Daniel 7:22, 27). The argument runs from the greater to the lesser: if the world is your docket, a property dispute is beneath you.\f*?
\v 3 Do you not know that we will judge angels, let alone matters of this life?
\v 4 So if you have cases about matters of this life, do you seat as judges those who are counted as nothing in the assembly \add [— you hand the bench to men who count for nothing among us]\add*?
\v 5 I say this to your shame. Is there really no one wise among you who will be able to decide between his brothers \add [— you, of all people]\add*?
\v 6 But brother goes to law with brother, and this before unbelievers?
\s1 Why Not Rather Be Wronged?
\p
\v 7 Already it is a complete defeat for you that you have lawsuits with one another \add [— before the verdict is read, you have lost]\add*. Why not rather be wronged? Why not rather be defrauded?
\v 8 But you yourselves wrong and defraud — and that, your brothers.
\s1 Such Were Some of You
\p
\v 9 Or do you not know that the unrighteous will not inherit God's kingdom? Do not be led astray: neither the sexually immoral, nor idolaters, nor adulterers, nor the soft, nor men who bed males\f + \fr 6:9 \ft "The soft" is Greek \tl malakoi\tl*, a word for the yielding partner. "Men who bed males" is \tl arsenokoitai\tl*, a compound apparently coined from the Greek of Leviticus 18:22 and 20:13, "you shall not lie with a male as with a woman."\f*,
\v 10 nor thieves, nor the greedy, nor drunkards, nor revilers, nor swindlers will inherit God's kingdom \add [— sexual sins and money sins and mouth sins, all on the same footing]\add*.
\v 11 And some of you were these things \add [— that is where this assembly was recruited from]\add*. But you were washed, but you were made holy, but you were set right in the name of the Lord Jesus Christ and in the Spirit of our God.
\s1 "All Things Are Permitted Me"
\p
\v 12 "All things are permitted me"\f + \fr 6:12 \ft A slogan of theirs, quoted back at them. They had taken freedom from the law as freedom from consequence. He does not deny the slogan; he puts two limits on it.\f* — but not all things are good for me. "All things are permitted me" — but I will not be brought under the authority of anything \add [— a man who cannot say no to an appetite is not free but owned]\add*.
\v 13 "Food for the belly, and the belly for food" \add [— another of your sayings, meant to make bodily appetite its own justification]\add* — and God will bring both to nothing. But the body is not for sexual immorality but for the Lord, and the Lord for the body.
\v 14 And God both raised the Lord and will raise us up through his power \add [— the body has a future, so it is not throwaway ground]\add*.
\s1 Your Body Belongs to the Lord
\p
\v 15 Do you not know that your bodies are members of Christ \add [i.e. limbs of his own body]\add*? Shall I then take the members of the Christ and make them members of a prostitute? Never!
\v 16 Or do you not know that the one who is joined to a prostitute is one body with her? For it says, "The two will become one flesh."\f + \fr 6:16 \ft Genesis 2:24. What scripture says of marriage holds of the act itself: the union is made whatever the setting, which is exactly why this one cannot be treated as a transaction that leaves nothing behind.\f*
\v 17 But the one joined to the Lord is one spirit with him \add [— and the two joinings will not sit together]\add*.
\v 18 Flee sexual immorality\f + \fr 6:18 \ft The word is flight, not resistance — Joseph running out of the house and leaving his cloak behind (Genesis 39:12).\f*. Every other sin a man commits is outside the body, but the one who is sexually immoral sins against his own body.
\v 19 Or do you not know that your body is a sanctuary of the Holy Spirit who is in you, whom you have from God, and that you are not your own?
\v 20 For you were bought with a price\f + \fr 6:20 \ft The language of the market: a price paid, and ownership transferred. Behind it stands God as Israel's redeemer, buying back what had been sold (Isaiah 43:1; 52:3). Compare 5:7 for the price.\f*. So glorify God in your body.

\c 7
\s1 Husbands and Wives
\p
\v 1 Now concerning the things you wrote about\f + \fr 7:1 \ft From here the letter works through the Corinthians' own written questions, each marked "Now concerning" (7:1, 25; 8:1; 12:1; 16:1, 12).\f*: "It is good for a man not to touch a woman."\f + \fr 7:1 \ft Another slogan of theirs, quoted before it is answered. Some in Corinth had turned Paul's teaching about the body into a rule of total abstinence, binding even on the married. "Touch" is a common euphemism for sexual relations (Genesis 20:6; Proverbs 6:29).\f*
\v 2 But because of sexual immorality, let each man have his own wife and each woman her own husband \add [— a rule that outruns people's capacity will produce the very thing it was meant to prevent]\add*.
\v 3 Let the husband give the wife what he owes her\f + \fr 7:3 \ft A debt, in the plain sense. Jewish law already required a husband to provide his wife with food, clothing and conjugal right (Exodus 21:10); what is startling here is that the obligation is stated in both directions.\f*, and likewise the wife the husband.
\v 4 The wife does not have authority over her own body, but the husband does; and likewise the husband does not have authority over his own body, but the wife does \add [— neither of you is your own any longer, and each has handed the other the deed]\add*.
\v 5 Do not defraud one another\f + \fr 7:5 \ft The same verb used at 6:7-8 for cheating a brother out of what is his. Withholding is put in that category.\f*, except perhaps by agreement for a season, so that you may have leisure for prayer, and then be together again, so that Satan may not test you because of your lack of self-control.
\v 6 But I say this as a concession, not as a command.
\v 7 I wish all men were as I myself am. But each has his own gift from God, one of this kind, another of that \add [— marriage and singleness are both gifts, and neither is the higher rung]\add*.
\s1 To the Unmarried and the Widows
\p
\v 8 Now I say to the unmarried and to the widows: it is good for them if they remain as I am.
\v 9 But if they cannot exercise self-control, let them marry; for it is better to marry than to burn \add [— a life spent on fire is no achievement]\add*.
\s1 To the Married, and to Those Married to Unbelievers
\p
\v 10 To the married I give this charge — not I but the Lord\f + \fr 7:10 \ft Paul is careful all through this chapter to say where each instruction comes from. Here he can cite the Lord's own teaching on divorce (Mark 10:2-12); at verse 12 and verse 25 he cannot, and says so.\f* — that a wife is not to be separated from her husband
\v 11 (but if she is separated, let her remain unmarried, or be reconciled to her husband) — and that a husband is not to send his wife away.
\v 12 To the rest I say — I, not the Lord \add [— the Lord did not address this case; the judgment is mine]\add* — if any brother has a wife who does not trust, and she is content to live with him, let him not send her away.
\v 13 And if any woman has a husband who does not trust, and he is content to live with her, let her not send her husband away.
\v 14 For the unbelieving husband has been made holy in the wife, and the unbelieving wife has been made holy in the brother; otherwise your children would be unclean, but as it is they are holy\f + \fr 7:14 \ft The argument runs from what they already grant to what they are disputing. They do not treat the children of such a marriage as unclean; the same holiness that reaches the child reaches the spouse. Holiness here spreads outward through the household rather than being contaminated from outside.\f*.
\v 15 But if the unbelieving one separates, let him separate. The brother or the sister is not bound as a slave in such cases; God has called you in peace.
\v 16 For how do you know, wife, whether you will rescue your husband? Or how do you know, husband, whether you will rescue your wife \add [— do not chain yourself to an outcome no one can promise you]\add*?
\s1 Remain as You Were Called
\p
\v 17 Only, as the Lord has apportioned to each, as God has called each — so let him walk. And this is what I lay down in all the assemblies.
\v 18 Was anyone called already circumcised? Let him not pull the foreskin back over it\f + \fr 7:18 \ft A real practice: Jews who wanted to pass in Greek society underwent surgery to disguise their circumcision (1 Maccabees 1:15).\f*. Has anyone been called uncircumcised? Let him not be circumcised.
\v 19 Circumcision is nothing and uncircumcision is nothing; what counts is keeping God's commands \add [— and circumcision was itself one of them, which shows what kind of thing he is asking for]\add*.
\v 20 Let each remain in the calling in which he was called.
\v 21 Were you called as a slave? Do not let it trouble you; but if you can indeed become free, rather make use of it \add [— take the opening if it comes]\add*.
\v 22 For the one called in the Lord as a slave is the Lord's freedman\f + \fr 7:22 \ft A \tl apeleutheros\tl* was a slave formally set free, who then stood in a lasting bond of loyalty to the master who freed him. A believing slave is that, to Christ — whatever his standing in law.\f*; likewise the one called as a free man is Christ's slave.
\v 23 You were bought with a price; do not become slaves of men \add [— which is what "I am of Paul" makes of you]\add*.
\v 24 Brothers, let each remain with God in the state in which he was called.
\s1 Concerning Virgins
\p
\v 25 Now concerning virgins I have no command of the Lord, but I give a judgment as one who has been shown mercy by the Lord to be faithful.
\v 26 I think, then, that this is good because of the distress that is upon us\f + \fr 7:26 \ft Greek \tl anankē\tl*, hard pressure — the constraint of a time of crisis. He does not say what it is. Everything practical in the rest of the chapter is weighed against it.\f*: that it is good for a man to remain as he is.
\v 27 Are you bound to a wife? Do not seek to be loosed. Are you loosed from a wife? Do not seek a wife.
\v 28 But if you do marry, you have not sinned; and if a virgin marries, she has not sinned \add [— hear that plainly: marriage is not a sin]\add*. Yet such will have pressure in the flesh, and I would spare you that.
\v 29 This I say, brothers: the time has been drawn in short. From now on, let those who have wives be as though they had none \add [— not leaving them, but holding everything with an open hand]\add*,
\v 30 and those who weep as though not weeping, and those who rejoice as though not rejoicing, and those who buy as though not holding on to it,
\v 31 and those who use the world as though not using it up; for the shape of this world is passing by \add [— the whole present arrangement is a procession already going past]\add*.
\s1 Undivided Before the Lord
\p
\v 32 I want you to be free from anxiety. The unmarried man is anxious about the things of the Lord, how he may please the Lord;
\v 33 but the married man is anxious about the things of the world, how he may please his wife \add [— not sinful things: the ordinary weight of keeping a household]\add*,
\v 34 and he is pulled in two. And the unmarried woman, the virgin, is anxious about the things of the Lord, that she may be holy in body and in spirit; but the married woman is anxious about the things of the world, how she may please her husband.
\v 35 I say this for your own benefit, not to throw a noose over you\f + \fr 7:35 \ft Greek \tl brochos\tl*, a slip-knot or snare. He will not turn his own preference into a trap for people who do not share it.\f*, but for what is fitting and for waiting on the Lord without being pulled away.
\s1 A Man and His Betrothed
\p
\v 36 If anyone thinks he is behaving improperly toward his virgin \add [i.e. toward the woman he is pledged to marry]\add*, if she is past the flower of her age and it ought to be so, let him do what he wants; he does not sin — let them marry.
\v 37 But whoever stands settled in his heart, being under no compulsion, and has authority over his own will, and has decided this in his own heart, to keep her as his virgin — he will do well.
\v 38 So the one who marries his virgin does well, and the one who does not marry her will do better \add [— better in this present pressure, not more righteous]\add*.
\s1 A Widow Is Free
\p
\v 39 A wife is bound for as long as her husband lives; but if the husband falls asleep\f + \fr 7:39 \ft "Falls asleep" is the ordinary way these letters speak of a believer's death; sleep implies waking. It runs all through chapter 15.\f*, she is free to be married to whom she wishes, only in the Lord.
\v 40 But she is happier if she remains as she is — in my judgment; and I think that I too have God's Spirit \add [— whatever others in Corinth are claiming for their own advice]\add*.

\c 8
\s1 Knowledge Puffs Up, Love Builds Up
\p
\v 1 Now concerning food sacrificed to idols\f + \fr 8:1 \ft Most meat in a Greek city passed through a temple. The question was practical and social: whether a believer could eat at the temple dining rooms where guilds met and families held their celebrations, and whether meat from the market was compromised.\f*: we know that "we all have knowledge."\f + \fr 8:1 \ft Their claim, quoted. Verse 7 will contradict its "all."\f* Knowledge puffs up, but love builds up \add [— a bladder swells; a building goes up]\add*.
\v 2 If anyone thinks he has come to know something, he does not yet know as he ought to know.
\v 3 But if anyone loves God, this one is known by him \add [— and being known by him is the thing that settles the matter]\add*.
\s1 One God, One Lord
\p
\v 4 So concerning the eating of food sacrificed to idols: we know that "an idol is nothing in the world," and that "there is no God but one."\f + \fr 8:4 \ft Both statements are the plain teaching of Israel's scriptures — the idol is the work of a man's hands, with a mouth that cannot speak (Psalm 115:4-8; Isaiah 44:9-20), and "the LORD our God, the LORD is one" (Deuteronomy 6:4). Paul does not dispute a word of it.\f*
\v 5 For even if there are so-called gods, whether in heaven or on earth — as indeed there are many gods and many lords \add [— the city is full of them, and men do experience them as powers]\add* —
\v 6 yet for us there is one God, the Father, out of whom are all things and we for him; and one Lord, Jesus Christ, through whom are all things and we through him\f + \fr 8:6 \ft The Shema of Deuteronomy 6:4 restated with the Son inside it: the Father as source and goal, the Lord Jesus as the one through whom the source acts. "Lord" is the word the Greek scriptures use for the divine name.\f*.
\s1 Do Not Destroy the Brother Christ Died For
\p
\v 7 But this knowledge is not in everyone. Some, being used to the idol until now, eat the food as truly sacrificed to an idol, and their conscience, being weak, is defiled \add [— they spent their whole lives in that world, and the reflex has not gone]\add*.
\v 8 Food will not present us to God; we are no worse off if we do not eat, and no better off if we do.
\v 9 But watch out that this right of yours\f + \fr 8:9 \ft Greek \tl exousia\tl*, a right or liberty a man is entitled to exercise. The word governs the whole of chapter 9, where Paul lists his own rights and then gives them all away.\f* does not somehow become something the weak strike their foot against.
\v 10 For if someone sees you, who have knowledge, reclining at table in an idol's temple, will not his conscience, weak as he is, be built up to eat food sacrificed to idols \add [i.e. talked into doing what he still believes is idolatry]\add*?
\v 11 So the weak one is destroyed by your knowledge — the brother for whom Christ died \add [— your freedom undoing what the cross was for]\add*.
\v 12 And sinning like this against the brothers and striking their weak conscience, you sin against Christ.
\v 13 Therefore, if food trips up my brother, I will never eat meat again \add [— not this meat: any meat, for good]\add*, so that I may not trip up my brother.

\c 9
\s1 The Rights I Have as One Sent
\p
\v 1 Am I not free? Am I not one sent? Have I not seen Jesus our Lord\f + \fr 9:1 \ft Having seen the risen Lord was the qualification for the office. Paul describes his own seeing at 15:8.\f*? Are you not my work in the Lord?
\v 2 If to others I am not one sent, at least I am to you; for you are the seal\f + \fr 9:2 \ft A \tl sphragis\tl* was the stamped impression that certified a document as genuine. Their own existence is the certificate; to doubt his commission is to doubt their own conversion.\f* of my sending in the Lord.
\v 3 This is my defense to those who examine me.
\v 4 Do we not have a right to eat and drink \add [i.e. to be fed at the expense of those we serve]\add*?
\v 5 Do we not have a right to take along a sister as wife, as also the rest of those sent, and the brothers of the Lord, and Cephas \add [— they all do it, and no one calls it a fault]\add*?
\v 6 Or is it only Barnabas and I who have no right to stop working \add [i.e. to put down the tools and be supported]\add*?
\s1 The Worker Deserves His Living
\p
\v 7 Who ever serves as a soldier at his own expense? Who plants a vineyard and does not eat its fruit? Or who shepherds a flock and does not eat from the milk of the flock?
\v 8 Am I saying these things only the way men do, or does the Torah not say the same?
\v 9 For in the Torah of Moses it is written: "You shall not muzzle an ox while it treads out the grain."\f + \fr 9:9 \ft Deuteronomy 25:4. The animal walking the threshing floor is not to be stopped from eating what it is threshing.\f* Is it about oxen that God is concerned?
\v 10 Or does he say it entirely for our sake \add [— if the working ox may eat, how much more the working man]\add*? Yes, it was written for our sake, because the plowman ought to plow in hope, and the thresher to thresh in hope of having a share.
\v 11 If we sowed spiritual things among you, is it too much if we reap things of the flesh from you \add [— the trade is hardly in my favor]\add*?
\v 12 If others share this right over you, do we not more? But we did not use this right; instead we bear all things, so that we may not put any obstacle in the way of the good news of the Christ.
\v 13 Do you not know that those who work at the holy things eat from the temple, and those who attend at the altar share with the altar\f + \fr 9:13 \ft The priests and Levites lived from the offerings, by God's own arrangement (Numbers 18:8-24; Deuteronomy 18:1-5). It was not charity but a right.\f*?
\v 14 In the same way the Lord laid it down that those who announce the good news should live from the good news\f + \fr 9:14 \ft The Lord's own instruction when he sent out his workers: "the laborer deserves his wages" (Luke 10:7).\f*.
\s1 I Have Used None of These Rights
\p
\v 15 But I have made use of none of these. And I am not writing this so that it may be done for me — for it would be better for me to die than — no one will empty me of my boast \add [— the sentence breaks off; he cannot finish it calmly]\add*!
\v 16 For if I announce the good news, that is no boast of mine; a compulsion is laid on me. Woe to me if I do not announce the good news\f + \fr 9:16 \ft The prophets' predicament: "there is in my heart a burning fire shut up in my bones, and I am weary with holding it in, and I cannot" (Jeremiah 20:9). The commission was not his idea and cannot be handed back.\f*!
\v 17 For if I do this willingly, I have wages; but if unwillingly, I have been entrusted with a stewardship.
\v 18 What then are my wages? That in announcing the good news I may set it out free of charge \add [— my pay is refusing pay]\add*, so as not to use up my right in the good news.
\s1 I Made Myself a Slave to All
\p
\v 19 For being free from all, I made myself a slave to all, so that I might gain the more.
\v 20 To the Jews I became as a Jew, so that I might gain Jews; to those under Torah as under Torah — though I myself am not under Torah — so that I might gain those under Torah;
\v 21 to those without Torah as without Torah — not being without God's law but within the Christ's law \add [— I have not left every jurisdiction; I have changed jurisdictions]\add* — so that I might gain those without Torah.
\v 22 To the weak I became weak, so that I might gain the weak \add [— the very thing I have just asked of you over the meat]\add*. I have become all things to all men, so that by all means I might rescue some.
\v 23 And I do all things for the sake of the good news, so that I may become a fellow sharer in it \add [— not only its herald but one of the people it saves]\add*.
\s1 Run to Win
\p
\v 24 Do you not know that those who run in the stadium\f + \fr 9:24 \ft Corinth hosted the Isthmian games, one of the four great festivals of Greece. Every image in these verses was familiar in the city.\f* all run, but one receives the prize? Run in such a way as to take hold of it.
\v 25 Every man who competes exercises self-control in all things \add [— his diet, his sleep, his whole life, for years]\add* — they to receive a crown that perishes\f + \fr 9:25 \ft The Isthmian victor's wreath was of pine or wild celery. It withered within days.\f*, but we one that does not.
\v 26 So then, I run — not without a mark to run at; I box — not as one beating the air.
\v 27 But I strike my own body under the eye\f + \fr 9:27 \ft A boxer's phrase: to land a blow beneath the eye. He treats his own body as the opponent in the ring.\f* and lead it away as a slave, so that after proclaiming to others I myself should not be found to fail the test\f + \fr 9:27 \ft Greek \tl adokimos\tl*, what a tested coin is called when the metal proves false — rejected after examination, disqualified. In the games, the herald who announced the contest was not thereby exempt from it.\f*.

\c 10
\s1 Our Fathers Had Everything and Fell
\p
\v 1 For I do not want you to be unaware, brothers, that our fathers\f + \fr 10:1 \ft He is writing to a largely non-Jewish assembly and calls the exodus generation "our fathers." Their story is this community's own story, and so is the warning in it.\f* were all under the cloud and all went through the sea,
\v 2 and all were immersed into Moses in the cloud and in the sea \add [— their own baptism, and it made them a people]\add*,
\v 3 and all ate the same spiritual food \add [i.e. the manna, bread rained down from heaven]\add*,
\v 4 and all drank the same spiritual drink; for they were drinking from a spiritual rock that followed them, and the rock was the Christ\f + \fr 10:4 \ft Water came from the rock at the start of the wilderness years and again near their end (Exodus 17:1-7; Numbers 20:2-13), and the tradition understood the rock to have travelled with them. "Rock" is one of God's own titles in the Song of Moses (Deuteronomy 32:4, 15, 18).\f*.
\v 5 But with most of them God was not pleased, for they were strewn down in the wilderness\f + \fr 10:5 \ft Five times "all," and then this. Of the whole generation that came out of Egypt, two men entered the land (Numbers 14:29-30). The word rendered "strewn down" pictures bodies scattered across the desert floor.\f*.
\s1 These Things Were Written for Us
\p
\v 6 These things became patterns for us, so that we should not be men who crave evil things, as they craved\f + \fr 10:6 \ft The craving for meat in the wilderness, after which the place was named Graves of Craving because they buried there the people who craved (Numbers 11:4, 33-34).\f*.
\v 7 Do not become idolaters, as some of them were; as it is written, "The people sat down to eat and drink, and rose up to play."\f + \fr 10:7 \ft Exodus 32:6, the golden calf. Aaron had announced it as a feast to the LORD, and the people may well have thought they were honoring him. It was idolatry all the same — and it took the form of a meal.\f*
\v 8 Nor let us commit sexual immorality, as some of them did, and twenty-three thousand fell in a single day\f + \fr 10:8 \ft Baal Peor (Numbers 25:1-9), where an invitation to the sacrificial meals of Moab led straight to bowing before its gods and to sexual sin. The meal and the idolatry were one act.\f*.
\v 9 Nor let us put the Christ to the test, as some of them tested him and were destroyed by the snakes\f + \fr 10:9 \ft Numbers 21:4-9. Testing God is demanding that he prove himself, and treating his provision with contempt.\f*.
\v 10 Nor grumble, as some of them grumbled and were destroyed by the Destroyer\f + \fr 10:10 \ft The grumbling after the spies' report, and after Korah (Numbers 14:2; 16:41-49). The Destroyer is the agent of the plague that struck Egypt's firstborn (Exodus 12:23).\f*.
\v 11 These things happened to them as patterns, and they were written to put us in mind — us, upon whom the ends of the ages have arrived \add [— we stand where the whole story has been heading]\add*.
\v 12 So let the one who thinks he stands watch out that he does not fall \add [— they had every reason to think it too]\add*.
\v 13 No testing has taken hold of you but what is common to man. And God is faithful, who will not let you be tested beyond what you are able, but with the testing will also make the way out, so that you are able to bear it \add [— he makes the way out; you still have to take it]\add*.
\s1 You Cannot Share Two Tables
\p
\v 14 Therefore, my beloved, flee from idolatry.
\v 15 I speak as to sensible men; judge for yourselves what I say.
\v 16 The cup of blessing\f + \fr 10:16 \ft The cup over which the thanksgiving was said at a Jewish festal meal.\f* that we bless — is it not a sharing\f + \fr 10:16 \ft Greek \tl koinōnia\tl*: not standing near a thing but having a real part in it. The word carries the whole argument that follows — three tables, three sharings, all of them real.\f* in the blood of the Christ? The bread that we break — is it not a sharing in the body of the Christ?
\v 17 Because there is one loaf, we who are many are one body; for we all share in the one loaf.
\v 18 Look at Israel according to flesh: are not those who eat the sacrifices sharers in the altar\f + \fr 10:18 \ft The priests and the worshipers ate their portions of the offering (Leviticus 7:6, 14-15). The meal completed the sacrifice; it was not a separate social occasion.\f*?
\v 19 What am I saying, then? That food sacrificed to an idol is anything, or that an idol is anything \add [— have I gone back on what I granted you?]\add*?
\v 20 No — but that what they sacrifice, they sacrifice to demons and not to God\f + \fr 10:20 \ft Deuteronomy 32:17, from the Song of Moses: "they sacrificed to demons and not to God, to gods they had never known." The image is nothing; what stands behind it is not nothing.\f*; and I do not want you to become sharers with demons.
\v 21 You cannot drink the Lord's cup and the cup of demons; you cannot share in the Lord's table and the table of demons \add [— not "must not": cannot. The two will not hold together]\add*.
\v 22 Or are we provoking the Lord to jealousy\f + \fr 10:22 \ft The same Song again: "they made me jealous with what is no god" (Deuteronomy 32:21). He is asking whether they intend to repeat the wilderness on purpose.\f*? Are we stronger than he?
\s1 What to Do in the Market and at a Neighbor's Table
\p
\v 23 "All things are permitted" — but not all things are good for us. "All things are permitted" — but not all things build up.
\v 24 Let no one seek his own good, but that of the other.
\v 25 Eat anything sold in the meat market\f + \fr 10:25 \ft The \tl macellum\tl*, the city's meat market, where the surplus from temple sacrifices was commonly sold on.\f*, examining nothing for conscience' sake \add [— do not interrogate the butcher]\add*;
\v 26 for "the earth is the Lord's, and its fullness."\f + \fr 10:26 \ft Psalm 24:1. No rite performed over an animal changes whose world it came from.\f*
\v 27 If one of the unbelievers invites you and you want to go, eat whatever is set before you, examining nothing for conscience' sake.
\v 28 But if someone says to you, "This has been offered in sacrifice,"\f + \fr 10:28 \ft The speaker uses the word a pagan would use — a holy offering, not an "idol-sacrifice." He means it. That is what changes the situation: eating now says something to him about where you stand.\f* do not eat — for the sake of the one who pointed it out, and for conscience' sake.
\v 29 I mean not your own conscience, but the other man's. For why is my freedom judged by another's conscience \add [— I know the objection; you have made it already]\add*?
\v 30 If I share in it with thanks, why am I spoken against for what I give thanks for?
\s1 Do Everything for God's Glory
\p
\v 31 So whether you eat or drink or whatever you do, do all things for the glory of God.
\v 32 Give no one cause to strike his foot — not Jews, not Greeks, not the assembly of God —
\v 33 just as I also please all men in all things, not seeking my own benefit but that of the many, so that they may be rescued.

\c 11
\s1 Imitate Me
\p
\v 1 Become imitators of me, as I also am of Christ \add [— the chain runs from him, through me, to you: each one giving up what he had a right to]\add*.
\s1 Covered and Uncovered Heads
\p
\v 2 I praise you because you remember me in everything and hold fast the traditions just as I handed them over to you\f + \fr 11:2 \ft "Handed over" and "received" are the formal words for passing on authorized teaching, teacher to student. They return at verse 23 and at 15:3.\f*.
\v 3 But I want you to know that the head of every man is the Christ, and the head of a woman is the man, and the head of the Christ is God\f + \fr 11:3 \ft "Head" here carries both senses the word could bear: the one a thing comes from, and the one who stands over it. Note the order — the chain does not run downward from God to woman; it begins with Christ and man and ends with God and Christ, so that the middle pair sits inside a larger frame. Christ himself has a head.\f*.
\v 4 Every man who prays or prophesies with something hanging down over his head shames his head \add [— his own, and the one named above]\add*.
\v 5 But every woman who prays or prophesies\f + \fr 11:5 \ft That women pray and prophesy aloud in the assembly is assumed here without argument. What is at issue is not whether but how.\f* with her head uncovered shames her head; for it is one and the same thing as being shaved\f + \fr 11:5 \ft A shaved head on a woman was a public disgrace, the mark of mourning, of a slave, or of a woman punished for adultery.\f*.
\v 6 For if a woman is not covered, let her also be shorn; but if it is shameful for a woman to be shorn or shaved, let her be covered.
\s1 Man and Woman in the Lord
\p
\v 7 For a man ought not to have his head covered, being the image and glory of God; but the woman is the glory of the man \add [— not his copy: his splendor]\add*.
\v 8 For man is not out of woman, but woman out of man\f + \fr 11:8 \ft Genesis 2:21-23, where the woman is built from the man's side.\f*;
\v 9 and indeed man was not created for the sake of the woman, but woman for the sake of the man\f + \fr 11:9 \ft Genesis 2:18: "it is not good for the man to be alone; I will make him a helper fit for him."\f*.
\v 10 For this reason the woman ought to have authority on her head\f + \fr 11:10 \ft The Greek says simply "authority" — her own. The covering is what authorizes her to pray and prophesy in the assembly, not a badge of someone else's power over her.\f*, because of the angels\f + \fr 11:10 \ft Worship was understood to be attended by the heavenly host: "before the angels I will sing your praise" (Psalm 138:1). The Qumran community excluded the blemished from its assembly "because the angels of holiness are among their congregation."\f*.
\v 11 Nevertheless, in the Lord woman is not apart from man, nor man apart from woman \add [— neither of you can do without the other]\add*;
\v 12 for just as the woman is out of the man, so also the man is through the woman \add [— that first order is reversed in every birth since]\add*; and all things are out of God.
\s1 Judge for Yourselves
\p
\v 13 Judge for yourselves: is it fitting for a woman to pray to God uncovered?
\v 14 Does not nature itself teach you that if a man wears his hair long it is a dishonor to him,
\v 15 but if a woman wears her hair long it is her glory? For her hair has been given her in place of a wrap \add [— nature has already covered her; the custom only carries that further]\add*.
\v 16 But if anyone is minded to be contentious — we have no such custom, nor do the assemblies of God \add [— whatever you may argue, no assembly anywhere does otherwise]\add*.
\s1 Your Gatherings Do More Harm Than Good
\p
\v 17 Now in giving this charge I do not praise you, because you come together not for the better but for the worse.
\v 18 For in the first place, when you come together as an assembly, I hear that there are tears among you \add [— the same word I used at the start of this letter]\add*, and in part I believe it.
\v 19 For there must even be factions among you, so that those who stand the test may become plain among you \add [— a split shows who is genuine, which is the only good in it]\add*.
\v 20 So when you come together in one place, it is not the Lord's supper that you eat \add [— whatever else that meal is, it is not his]\add*;
\v 21 for in eating, each one takes his own supper ahead of the others, and one goes hungry while another gets drunk\f + \fr 11:21 \ft The wealthy could arrive early and brought their own provisions; slaves and day laborers came when their work released them, to find the food gone. A Roman dining room seated a dozen at most, and the rest ate worse food in the courtyard. The city's ranking had simply walked into the assembly's meal.\f*.
\v 22 Do you not have houses to eat and drink in? Or do you despise the assembly of God and shame those who have nothing? What shall I say to you? Shall I praise you? In this I do not praise you.
\s1 What I Handed Over to You About the Supper
\p
\v 23 For I received from the Lord what I also handed over to you: that the Lord Jesus, on the night in which he was handed over, took bread,
\v 24 and when he had given thanks he broke it and said, "This is my body, which is for you. Do this as my memorial."\f + \fr 11:24 \ft The word is the one used of Passover (Exodus 12:14): a remembrance that does not merely think back on an event but keeps it and enters it. Israel does not recall the exodus at Passover; it eats the exodus.\f*
\v 25 In the same way also the cup, after supper, saying, "This cup is the new covenant in my blood\f + \fr 11:25 \ft The covenant promised in Jeremiah 31:31-34, when the old one lay broken. Sinai was sealed with blood thrown over the people — "behold the blood of the covenant" (Exodus 24:8); this one is sealed with his own.\f*. Do this, as often as you drink it, as my memorial."
\v 26 For as often as you eat this bread and drink the cup, you announce the Lord's death until he comes \add [— the meal is a public statement, and you make it every time]\add*.
\s1 Eating Without Discerning the Body
\p
\v 27 So whoever eats the bread or drinks the cup of the Lord in a way unworthy of it \add [— the manner is what is unworthy, not the man]\add* will be answerable for the body and the blood of the Lord.
\v 28 Let a man test himself, and so eat of the bread and drink of the cup \add [— test yourself and then eat; he does not say do not eat]\add*.
\v 29 For the one who eats and drinks without discerning the body\f + \fr 11:29 \ft "The body" stands without qualification here. It is the bread, and it is also the people at the table — the hungry ones included — whom chapter 12 will call the body of Christ. To fail to see them is to fail to see what the bread means.\f* eats and drinks judgment on himself.
\v 30 That is why many among you are weak and sick, and a good number have fallen asleep \add [— look around your own assembly; it has already begun]\add*.
\v 31 But if we discerned ourselves, we would not be judged.
\v 32 And when we are judged by the Lord, we are being disciplined\f + \fr 11:32 \ft The word for a father's training of a son: "as a man disciplines his son, so the LORD your God disciplines you" (Deuteronomy 8:5; Proverbs 3:11-12). What is falling on them is correction, and it is aimed at keeping them from the verdict the world receives.\f*, so that we may not be condemned along with the world.
\s1 Wait for One Another
\p
\v 33 So then, my brothers, when you come together to eat, wait for one another \add [— that is the whole remedy: wait]\add*.
\v 34 If anyone is hungry, let him eat at home, so that you do not come together for judgment. And the rest I will set in order whenever I come.

\c 12
\s1 No One Says "Jesus Is Lord" Except in the Spirit
\p
\v 1 Now concerning the things of the Spirit, brothers, I do not want you to be unaware.
\v 2 You know that when you were of the nations you were carried away to the voiceless idols\f + \fr 12:2 \ft "They have mouths, but do not speak" (Psalm 115:5). Their old worship was directed at things that could not answer, and it swept them along rather than being chosen.\f*, however you were led.
\v 3 So I make known to you that no one speaking in God's Spirit says, "Jesus is under the ban,"\f + \fr 12:3 \ft Greek \tl anathema\tl*, from Hebrew \tl cherem\tl*, the irrevocable ban of destruction (Leviticus 27:28-29; Joshua 6:17). The word returns at 16:22, turned the other way.\f* and no one can say, "Jesus is Lord," except in the Holy Spirit \add [— so every one of you who says it has the Spirit; there is no lower tier]\add*.
\s1 One Spirit, Many Gifts
\p
\v 4 There are different kinds of gifts\f + \fr 12:4 \ft Greek \tl charismata\tl*, from \tl charis\tl*, favor. They asked about "spiritual things"; he answers about grace-gifts. A gift given freely is no basis for ranking anyone.\f*, but the same Spirit;
\v 5 and there are different kinds of service, and the same Lord;
\v 6 and there are different kinds of workings, but the same God who works all of them in all.
\v 7 To each is given the showing of the Spirit for the common good \add [— to each, without exception, and for everyone's benefit, not the holder's]\add*.
\v 8 For to one is given through the Spirit a word of wisdom, and to another a word of knowledge according to the same Spirit \add [— your two favorite words, and both of them handed out by someone else]\add*,
\v 9 to another faithfulness by the same Spirit, to another gifts of healings by the one Spirit,
\v 10 to another workings of powers, to another prophecy, to another discernings of spirits, to another kinds of tongues, to another interpretation of tongues\f + \fr 12:10 \ft Tongues stands last here, and last again in the list at verse 28. If Corinth ranked it first, the placing is deliberate.\f*.
\v 11 But all these one and the same Spirit works, apportioning to each one individually as he wills \add [— so no one earned his, and no one was overlooked]\add*.
\s1 One Body, Many Members
\p
\v 12 For just as the body is one and has many members, and all the members of the body, though many, are one body, so also is the Christ\f + \fr 12:12 \ft He does not say "so also is the assembly." The community is named by the name of Christ himself. The body image was common in Greek and Roman politics, where it was normally used to tell the lower orders to keep their place; verses 22-24 turn it inside out.\f*.
\v 13 For in one Spirit we were all immersed into one body — whether Jews or Greeks, whether slaves or free — and we were all given one Spirit to drink \add [— the two divisions this world runs on, both of them inside the one body]\add*.
\v 14 For the body is not one member but many.
\v 15 If the foot should say, "Because I am not a hand, I am not of the body," it is not for that reason not of the body \add [— thinking you do not belong does not make it so]\add*.
\v 16 And if the ear should say, "Because I am not an eye, I am not of the body," it is not for that reason not of the body.
\v 17 If the whole body were an eye, where would the hearing be? If the whole were hearing, where would the smelling be \add [— a body that was all one organ would be a monster, not a marvel]\add*?
\v 18 But as it is, God set the members, each one of them, in the body just as he willed \add [— to want a different gift is to quarrel with the one who placed you]\add*.
\v 19 And if they were all one member, where would the body be?
\v 20 But as it is, there are many members, yet one body.
\s1 No Member Can Be Spared
\p
\v 21 The eye cannot say to the hand, "I have no need of you," nor again the head to the feet, "I have no need of you."
\v 22 On the contrary, the members of the body that seem to be weaker are necessary \add [— the hidden and fragile parts are the ones life depends on]\add*,
\v 23 and the parts of the body we think less honorable, these we clothe with greater honor, and our unpresentable parts are given greater modesty,
\v 24 while our presentable parts have no need of it. But God mixed the body together, giving greater honor to the part that lacked it \add [— he built the compensation in on purpose]\add*,
\v 25 so that there might be no tear in the body\f + \fr 12:25 \ft The same word for a tear that opened the letter (1:10) and named the trouble at the meal (11:18). The body was designed to make it impossible; Corinth has managed it anyway.\f*, but that the members might have the same care for one another.
\v 26 And if one member suffers, all the members suffer with it; if one member is honored, all the members rejoice with it \add [— which is exactly what did not happen at your supper]\add*.
\s1 God Set Them in the Assembly
\p
\v 27 Now you are Christ's body, and each of you a member of it.
\v 28 And God set in the assembly first those sent, second prophets, third teachers, then powers, then gifts of healings, helpings\f + \fr 12:28 \ft Plain practical assistance — taking hold of a burden for someone. Not a spectacular gift, and named alongside them.\f*, steerings\f + \fr 12:28 \ft From the word for the man at the ship's tiller: the unglamorous work of giving a community direction.\f*, kinds of tongues.
\v 29 Are all those sent? Are all prophets? Are all teachers? Do all work powers?
\v 30 Do all have gifts of healings? Do all speak in tongues? Do all interpret \add [— the answer to every one of these is no, and that is by design]\add*?
\v 31 But be zealous for the greater gifts. And I show you a way beyond all comparison.

\c 13
\s1 Without Love I Am Nothing
\p
\v 1 If I speak in the tongues of men and of angels\f + \fr 13:1 \ft He grants the highest claim anyone could make for the gift — that it is the speech of heaven — before emptying it.\f* but have no love, I have become sounding bronze or a clanging cymbal\f + \fr 13:1 \ft Cymbals were proper instruments in Israel's worship (Psalm 150:5), but struck alone they make noise, not music. Bronze and cymbals were also the racket of the ecstatic pagan rites the Corinthians had come out of.\f*.
\v 2 And if I have prophecy and know all the secrets and all knowledge, and if I have all faithfulness so as to move mountains, but have no love, I am nothing.
\v 3 And if I dole out all that I own, and if I hand over my body so that I may boast, but have no love, I gain nothing.
\s1 What Love Does
\p
\v 4 Love is long-tempered\f + \fr 13:4 \ft The word the Greek scriptures use of God himself: "merciful and gracious, slow to anger" (Exodus 34:6; Psalm 86:15). Everything in this list is first a description of God.\f*; love is kind. Love is not jealous; it does not brag, it is not puffed up \add [— the very word for what is wrong with you]\add*,
\v 5 it does not behave improperly, it does not seek its own, it is not provoked, it does not reckon up the evil done to it\f + \fr 13:5 \ft An accountant's word: it keeps no ledger of wrongs. God's own mercy is described the same way — "blessed is the man whose sin the LORD does not reckon" (Psalm 32:2).\f*,
\v 6 it does not rejoice at wrongdoing but rejoices with the truth.
\v 7 It covers all things, trusts all things, hopes all things, endures all things.
\s1 Love Never Falls
\p
\v 8 Love never falls. But if there are prophecies, they will be brought to nothing; if tongues, they will stop; if knowledge, it will be brought to nothing \add [— every gift you are ranking is temporary]\add*.
\v 9 For we know in part and we prophesy in part;
\v 10 but when the complete comes, the partial will be brought to nothing.
\v 11 When I was an infant, I spoke as an infant, I thought as an infant, I reasoned as an infant; when I became a man, I put away the things of the infant \add [— not wrong for a child; simply outgrown]\add*.
\v 12 For now we see through a mirror, in a riddle\f + \fr 13:12 \ft An ancient mirror was polished bronze: a real image, but indirect and imperfect. "In a riddle" is the phrase of Numbers 12:8, where God says he speaks to other prophets in riddles but to Moses face to face. What was Moses' alone is here promised to everyone.\f*, but then face to face. Now I know in part, but then I will know fully, just as I have been fully known \add [— he already knows me that way; one day the knowing will run both ways]\add*.
\v 13 And now these three remain: faithfulness, hope, love; but the greatest of these is love.

\c 14
\s1 Prophecy Builds Up, Tongues Do Not
\p
\v 1 Pursue love, and be zealous for the things of the Spirit, but especially that you may prophesy.
\v 2 For the one who speaks in a tongue does not speak to men but to God, for no one hears him \add [i.e. no one takes in a word of it]\add*; in the Spirit he speaks secrets.
\v 3 But the one who prophesies speaks to men — building up and encouragement and comfort.
\v 4 The one who speaks in a tongue builds himself up, but the one who prophesies builds up an assembly \add [— both are real; only one of them reaches anyone else]\add*.
\v 5 I want you all to speak in tongues, but more that you prophesy. Greater is the one who prophesies than the one who speaks in tongues — unless he interprets, so that the assembly may receive building up.
\s1 Sound Without Meaning
\p
\v 6 As it is, brothers, if I come to you speaking in tongues, what good will I do you, unless I speak to you in an uncovering or in knowledge or in prophecy or in teaching?
\v 7 Even lifeless things that give a sound — whether flute or lyre — if they give no distinction in the notes, how will what is played on the flute or the lyre be known?
\v 8 And if the trumpet gives an unclear sound, who will get ready for battle \add [— an army that cannot read the call cannot fight]\add*?
\v 9 So also you: unless with the tongue you give a word that can be recognized, how will what is spoken be known? You will be speaking into the air.
\v 10 There are, it may be, so many kinds of languages in the world, and none is without meaning.
\v 11 So if I do not know the force of the language, I will be a foreigner to the one speaking, and the one speaking a foreigner to me \add [— strangers to each other, inside one room]\add*.
\v 12 So also you: since you are zealous for spirits, seek to abound in them for the building up of the assembly \add [— I do not ask you to want less, but to aim it somewhere]\add*.
\s1 Pray With the Mind as Well
\p
\v 13 So let the one who speaks in a tongue pray that he may interpret.
\v 14 For if I pray in a tongue, my spirit prays, but my mind is without fruit \add [— genuine prayer, but it never passes through my understanding]\add*.
\v 15 What then? I will pray with the spirit, but I will pray with the mind also; I will sing with the spirit, but I will sing with the mind also \add [— both, not one instead of the other]\add*.
\v 16 Otherwise, if you bless with the spirit, how will the one who fills the place of the outsider say the "Amen"\f + \fr 14:16 \ft Hebrew \tl amen\tl*, "it stands firm" — the congregation's word of ratification, said back to a prayer to make it their own (Nehemiah 8:6). A man cannot ratify what he did not understand.\f* at your thanksgiving, since he does not know what you are saying?
\v 17 For you give thanks well enough, but the other man is not built up \add [— your prayer is fine; it simply leaves everyone else outside]\add*.
\v 18 I thank God that I speak in tongues more than all of you \add [— I am not ruling on a gift I lack]\add*;
\v 19 but in an assembly I would rather speak five words with my mind, so that I may instruct others also, than ten thousand words in a tongue.
\s1 What an Outsider Sees When He Walks In
\p
\v 20 Brothers, do not be children in your thinking; be infants in evil, but in your thinking be full-grown.
\v 21 In the Torah it is written: "By men of other tongues and by the lips of strangers I will speak to this people, and not even so will they listen to me, says the Lord."\f + \fr 14:21 \ft Isaiah 28:11-12. Israel's leaders had mocked the prophet's plain word as fit only for babies, so God said he would address them instead through the incomprehensible speech of foreign invaders — and they still would not hear. In the prophets, unintelligible speech is a sign of judgment on people who refused the clear word, not a means of winning them.\f*
\v 22 So tongues are for a sign, not to those who trust but to unbelievers; but prophecy is not for unbelievers but for those who trust.
\v 23 So if the whole assembly comes together in one place and all speak in tongues, and outsiders or unbelievers come in, will they not say that you are out of your minds\f + \fr 14:23 \ft The word is the one used for the frenzy of the ecstatic cults. A Corinthian walking in off the street would file what he saw under the rites he already knew, and walk out again.\f*?
\v 24 But if all prophesy, and some unbeliever or outsider comes in, he is convicted by all, examined by all;
\v 25 the hidden things of his heart become plain, and so, falling on his face, he will bow down to God and declare that God is really among you\f + \fr 14:25 \ft The prophets' own picture of the nations recognizing the God of Israel: "God is with you only, and there is no other" (Isaiah 45:14; compare Zechariah 8:23).\f*.
\s1 Order in the Assembly
\p
\v 26 What then, brothers? When you come together, each one has a psalm, has a teaching, has an uncovering, has a tongue, has an interpretation \add [— everyone arrives with something; that much is right]\add*. Let everything be for building up.
\v 27 If anyone speaks in a tongue, let it be two, or at most three, and each in turn, and let one interpret.
\v 28 But if there is no interpreter, let him keep silent in the assembly, and speak to himself and to God \add [— the gift is not forbidden him, only the floor]\add*.
\v 29 Let two or three prophets speak, and let the others discern \add [— prophecy is to be weighed, not swallowed]\add*.
\v 30 But if something is uncovered to another sitting there, let the first keep silent.
\v 31 For you can all prophesy one by one, so that all may learn and all be encouraged.
\v 32 And the spirits of prophets are subject to prophets \add [— no one can plead that the Spirit left him no choice]\add*;
\v 33 for God is not a God of disorder but of peace. As in all the assemblies of the holy ones,
\s1 Silence, and Who Gave the Word of God
\p
\v 34 let the women keep silent in the assemblies\f + \fr 14:34 \ft This instruction sits awkwardly beside 11:5, where women pray and prophesy without objection, and beside verse 31 here, where "all" may prophesy in turn. "Keep silent" is the same word used of the tongue-speaker in verse 28 and the prophet in verse 30, and in both of those it means silence in a particular situation, not silence altogether. Verse 35 suggests what is in view: questions asked across the room while prophecy was being weighed. Some old manuscripts place verses 34-35 after verse 40, which shows the difficulty was felt early. Some read the two verses as a Corinthian ruling that verse 36 then knocks down.\f*; for it is not permitted them to speak, but let them be subject, as the Torah also says.
\v 35 And if they want to learn something, let them ask their own husbands at home; for it is shameful for a woman to speak in an assembly.
\v 36 Or did the word of God go out from you? Or did it reach you alone \add [— you did not originate it and you are not its only address]\add*?
\s1 A Command of the Lord
\p
\v 37 If anyone thinks he is a prophet or a spiritual man, let him recognize that what I write to you is a command of the Lord.
\v 38 But if anyone does not recognize it, he is not recognized \add [— the man who will not acknowledge this forfeits the standing he claims]\add*.
\v 39 So then, my brothers, be zealous to prophesy, and do not forbid speaking in tongues \add [— I have ordered the gift, not banned it]\add*.
\v 40 But let everything be done in a fitting way and in order.

\c 15
\s1 The Good News I Handed Over to You
\p
\v 1 Now I make known to you, brothers, the good news that I announced to you, which you also received, in which you also stand,
\v 2 through which you are also being rescued — if you hold fast to the word I announced to you; unless you trusted for nothing \add [— and that is a real possibility, which is why I am writing this]\add*.
\v 3 For I handed over to you as of first importance what I also received: that Christ died for our sins according to the Scriptures\f + \fr 15:3 \ft "He was wounded for our transgressions... the LORD laid on him the iniquity of us all" (Isaiah 53:5-6).\f*,
\v 4 and that he was buried, and that he has been raised on the third day according to the Scriptures\f + \fr 15:4 \ft "On the third day he will raise us up, and we shall live before him" (Hosea 6:2); and the three days and nights of Jonah (Jonah 1:17).\f*,
\v 5 and that he was seen by Cephas, then by the Twelve.
\v 6 Then he was seen by more than five hundred brothers at one time, of whom most remain until now, though some have fallen asleep \add [— you can still go and ask them]\add*.
\v 7 Then he was seen by James, then by all those sent.
\v 8 And last of all, as to one born too soon\f + \fr 15:8 \ft Greek \tl ektrōma\tl*, a miscarriage — a birth out of its proper time and not properly formed. It is his own word for himself: he came into the office violently and out of sequence.\f*, he was seen by me also.
\v 9 For I am the least of those sent, not fit to be called one sent, because I hunted down the assembly of God.
\v 10 But by God's favor I am what I am, and his favor toward me did not turn out empty; rather I labored more than all of them — yet not I, but the favor of God with me.
\v 11 So whether it was I or they, this is what we proclaim, and this is what you trusted \add [— on this there is no party to choose between]\add*.
\s1 If the Dead Are Not Raised
\p
\v 12 Now if Christ is proclaimed as raised from among the dead, how do some among you say there is no resurrection of the dead\f + \fr 15:12 \ft They are not denying that something happened to Jesus. They are denying that dead bodies come back — an idea most educated Greeks found not merely unproven but absurd, since the point of dying was to be rid of the body. Paul answers that the two cannot be separated.\f*?
\v 13 If there is no resurrection of the dead, then neither has Christ been raised \add [— his rising is not a stray miracle but the first case of the thing you deny]\add*;
\v 14 and if Christ has not been raised, then our proclamation is empty and your faithfulness is empty.
\v 15 And we are found to be false witnesses of God, because we testified against God that he raised the Christ — whom he did not raise, if in fact the dead are not raised.
\v 16 For if the dead are not raised, neither has Christ been raised;
\v 17 and if Christ has not been raised, your faithfulness is worthless; you are still in your sins \add [— still, exactly where you were before you heard any of this]\add*.
\v 18 Then those also who have fallen asleep in Christ have perished.
\v 19 If in this life only we have hoped in Christ, we are of all men most to be pitied \add [— everyone else at least lived for something real]\add*.
\s1 Christ the Firstfruits
\p
\v 20 But as it is, Christ has been raised from among the dead, the firstfruits of those who have fallen asleep\f + \fr 15:20 \ft The first sheaf of the harvest, brought to the priest and waved before the LORD (Leviticus 23:10-11). It is not the whole crop but it is the same crop, and it is the pledge that the rest is coming.\f*.
\v 21 For since death came through a man, through a man also came the resurrection of the dead.
\v 22 For just as in Adam all die, so also in the Christ all will be made alive \add [— all who are in the one, all who are in the other; the next verse names them]\add*.
\v 23 But each in his own rank\f + \fr 15:23 \ft A military word for a division taking its appointed place in the column. The raising happens in stages, not all at once.\f*: Christ the firstfruits, then those who belong to the Christ at his arrival.
\v 24 Then the end, when he hands over the kingdom to the God and Father, when he has brought to nothing every rule and every authority and power.
\v 25 For he must reign until he has put all his enemies under his feet\f + \fr 15:25 \ft Psalm 110:1: "Sit at my right hand until I make your enemies a footstool for your feet." A conqueror set his foot on the neck of the defeated (Joshua 10:24).\f*.
\v 26 The last enemy to be brought to nothing is death \add [— last, because it is the weapon all the others use]\add*.
\v 27 For "he put all things in subjection under his feet."\f + \fr 15:27 \ft Psalm 8:6, which speaks of the man God crowned with glory and set over the works of his hands. Christ is that man.\f* But when it says that all things have been put in subjection, it is plain that this does not include the one who put all things in subjection under him.
\v 28 And when all things have been put in subjection to him, then the Son himself will also be put in subjection to the one who put all things in subjection to him, so that God may be all in all \add [— the Son's reign was for this, and it ends in this]\add*.
\s1 Why Take the Risk If There Is No Rising?
\p
\v 29 Otherwise, what will those do who are immersed on behalf of the dead\f + \fr 15:29 \ft A practice among some at Corinth that Paul neither commends nor condemns; he only points out that it makes no sense at all unless the dead have a future. What exactly it was is not explained.\f*? If the dead are not raised at all, why then are they immersed on their behalf?
\v 30 And why are we in danger every hour?
\v 31 I die daily — by the boast I have in you, brothers, in Christ Jesus our Lord!
\v 32 If, in the way men speak, I fought wild beasts at Ephesus\f + \fr 15:32 \ft Whether an actual arena or a fight for his life by other means, he does not say. Acts records a riot against him in that city (Acts 19:23-41).\f*, what did it gain me? If the dead are not raised, "let us eat and drink, for tomorrow we die."\f + \fr 15:32 \ft Isaiah 22:13, the words of Jerusalem under siege, feasting because it assumed it was finished anyway. If death is the end, they were right, and my whole life is a waste.\f*
\v 33 Do not be led astray: "Bad company ruins good character."\f + \fr 15:33 \ft A line from the playwright Menander, in circulation as a proverb. Who they listen to will shape how they live.\f*
\v 34 Sober up, as you ought, and stop sinning; for some have no knowledge of God \add [— among you who boast of knowledge]\add*. I say this to your shame.
\s1 With What Kind of Body?
\p
\v 35 But someone will say, "How are the dead raised? And with what kind of body do they come?"
\v 36 Senseless man! What you sow is not made alive unless it dies \add [— dying is how the thing gets started, not what stops it]\add*.
\v 37 And what you sow is not the body that will be, but a bare grain — of wheat, perhaps, or of some other seed \add [— a kernel looks nothing like a standing stalk]\add*.
\v 38 But God gives it a body as he willed, and to each of the seeds its own body.
\v 39 Not all flesh is the same flesh: there is one flesh of men, another flesh of beasts, another flesh of birds, another of fish.
\v 40 And there are heavenly bodies and earthly bodies; but the glory of the heavenly is one kind and the glory of the earthly another.
\v 41 There is one glory of the sun, and another glory of the moon, and another glory of the stars; for star differs from star in glory \add [— look at how many kinds of body he has already made]\add*.
\s1 Sown One Way, Raised Another
\p
\v 42 So also is the resurrection of the dead. It is sown in decay, it is raised without decay;
\v 43 it is sown in dishonor, it is raised in glory; it is sown in weakness, it is raised in power;
\v 44 it is sown a creaturely body, it is raised a spiritual body\f + \fr 15:44 \ft The contrast is not between solid and immaterial. Both are bodies. The first is run by ordinary creaturely life, fit for this age; the second is run by the Spirit, fit for the age to come. The same word "creaturely" described the man of 2:14.\f*. If there is a creaturely body, there is also a spiritual one.
\v 45 So also it is written: "The first man Adam became a living creature"\f + \fr 15:45 \ft Genesis 2:7: God breathed into him and the man became a living soul. Adam received life; the last Adam gives it.\f*; the last Adam became a life-giving spirit.
\v 46 But the spiritual is not first; the creaturely is, and then the spiritual \add [— the movement runs forward, not back to some earlier state]\add*.
\v 47 The first man is out of the earth, made of dust; the second man is out of heaven.
\v 48 As the man of dust is, so also are those made of dust; and as the heavenly man is, so also are those who are heavenly.
\v 49 And just as we have worn the image of the man of dust, let us also wear the image of the heavenly man.\f + \fr 15:49 \ft Nearly every early manuscript reads \tl phoresōmen\tl*, "let us wear" — a summons. A few read \tl phoresomen\tl*, "we shall wear" — a promise, and that is what most English translations print. One letter separates them, and in the first century the two were pronounced alike, so a scribe writing from dictation could not have heard which was said.\f*
\s1 We Will All Be Changed
\p
\v 50 This I say, brothers: flesh and blood cannot inherit God's kingdom\f + \fr 15:50 \ft "Flesh and blood" is the Hebrew way of saying a human being as he now is, made for this life. Such a body is not equipped to receive what is promised; it has to be changed.\f*, nor does decay inherit what is without decay.
\v 51 Look, I tell you a secret: we will not all fall asleep, but we will all be changed \add [— some of us will still be alive, and even we will not go in unaltered]\add* —
\v 52 in an instant, in the blink of an eye, at the last trumpet\f + \fr 15:52 \ft The trumpet that sounded at Sinai and that the prophets set at the day of the LORD (Exodus 19:16; Joel 2:1; Isaiah 27:13) — God's own signal that he has arrived.\f*. For the trumpet will sound, and the dead will be raised without decay, and we will be changed.
\v 53 For this perishable must put on what does not perish, and this mortal must put on what does not die \add [— put on, like a garment: the same body clothed, not swapped]\add*.
\v 54 And when this perishable puts on what does not perish, and this mortal puts on what does not die, then will come to pass the word that is written: "Death has been swallowed up in victory."\f + \fr 15:54 \ft Isaiah 25:8, spoken over God's feast for all peoples on his mountain: the devourer is itself devoured.\f*
\v 55 "Where, death, is your victory? Where, death, is your sting?"\f + \fr 15:55 \ft Hosea 13:14. The taunt is thrown at an enemy already beaten.\f*
\v 56 The sting of death is sin, and the power of sin is the Torah \add [— the law exposes the sin, sin turns the exposure into a sentence]\add*.
\v 57 But thanks be to God, who gives us the victory through our Lord Jesus Christ.
\s1 Your Labor Is Not Empty
\p
\v 58 So then, my beloved brothers, be settled, unshaken, always abounding in the work of the Lord, knowing that your labor is not empty in the Lord \add [— everything this letter has asked of you rests on that]\add*.

\c 16
\s1 The Collection for the Holy Ones
\p
\v 1 Now concerning the collection for the holy ones\f + \fr 16:1 \ft The relief fund Paul was gathering from the Gentile assemblies for the impoverished believers in Jerusalem. He explains the reasoning elsewhere: the nations have come to share in Israel's spiritual wealth, and owe her something material in return (Romans 15:25-27).\f*: as I laid it down for the assemblies of Galatia, so you also are to do \add [— you are not the only assembly doing this]\add*.
\v 2 On the first day of the week let each of you put something aside by himself, storing up whatever he has prospered in \add [— each man, in proportion to how his week went]\add*, so that collections need not be made when I come \add [i.e. so there is no scramble when I arrive]\add*.
\v 3 And when I arrive, whomever you approve I will send with letters\f + \fr 16:3 \ft Letters of accreditation, the ordinary way a traveller was vouched for by the community that sent him. Note that they choose the men, not Paul.\f* to carry your gift to Jerusalem.
\v 4 And if it is fitting that I go too, they will go with me.
\s1 My Travel Plans
\p
\v 5 I will come to you when I have passed through Macedonia — for I am passing through Macedonia \add [i.e. by the land route north and round, not straight across the sea]\add* —
\v 6 and perhaps I will stay with you, or even spend the winter\f + \fr 16:6 \ft Sea travel largely stopped from November to March. A man who arrived late in the year stayed until spring.\f*, so that you may send me on my way wherever I go.
\v 7 For I do not want to see you now just in passing; I hope to remain with you some time, if the Lord permits \add [— which is not a formality: my plans are his to change]\add*.
\v 8 But I will remain in Ephesus until Pentecost\f + \fr 16:8 \ft This is where he is writing from. He still keeps time by Israel's festival calendar, even writing to a largely Gentile city.\f*,
\v 9 for a wide door has been opened to me, and an effective one, and there are many who set themselves against me \add [— the opposition is fierce precisely because the work is going well]\add*.
\s1 Timothy and Apollos
\p
\v 10 If Timothy comes, see to it that he is with you without fear \add [— yes, fear: you have a way of sizing men up]\add*, for he is doing the Lord's work, as I am.
\v 11 So let no one count him as nothing. Send him on his way in peace, so that he may come to me; for I am waiting for him with the brothers.
\v 12 Now concerning Apollos the brother \add [— the brother, nothing more: not the head of a party]\add*: I strongly urged him to come to you with the brothers, but it was not at all his will to come now; he will come when he has opportunity.
\s1 Stand Firm, and Do Everything in Love
\p
\v 13 Keep watch, stand firm in the faithfulness, act like men, be strong\f + \fr 16:13 \ft "Act like men, be strong" is the pairing God speaks to Israel at her hardest thresholds — to Joshua before the land, to Solomon before the temple: "be strong and courageous" (Deuteronomy 31:6; Joshua 1:6, 9; 1 Chronicles 22:13).\f*.
\v 14 Let everything of yours be done in love \add [— every one of the four just named, or they turn into their opposites]\add*.
\s1 Give Recognition to the House of Stephanas
\p
\v 15 Now I call on you, brothers — you know the household of Stephanas, that they are the firstfruits of Achaia\f + \fr 16:15 \ft The first in the province to come to faith — the first sheaf, and the pledge of everyone who followed.\f* and have set themselves to serve the holy ones \add [— no one appointed them; they saw the need and posted themselves to it]\add* —
\v 16 that you also be subject to such men, and to everyone who works with them and labors \add [— that is where authority comes from here: not from polish, but from service]\add*.
\v 17 I rejoice at the arrival of Stephanas and Fortunatus and Achaicus\f + \fr 16:17 \ft Almost certainly the men who carried the Corinthians' letter to Ephesus, and who are carrying this one home.\f*, because they have filled up what was lacking from you \add [i.e. they stood in for you, since you could not come yourselves]\add*;
\v 18 for they refreshed my spirit and yours. So give recognition to such men.
\s1 Greetings
\p
\v 19 The assemblies of Asia greet you. Aquila and Prisca\f + \fr 16:19 \ft The tentmakers Paul lodged and worked with when he first came to Corinth, expelled from Rome and later moved on to Ephesus (Acts 18:2-3, 18-19). Old friends of this assembly.\f* greet you warmly in the Lord, together with the assembly in their house.
\v 20 All the brothers greet you. Greet one another with a holy kiss \add [— you, with each other]\add*.
\v 21 The greeting is in my own hand — Paul's\f + \fr 16:21 \ft Up to here a secretary has been writing. Paul takes the pen for the last lines, which is how a letter was authenticated. The Corinthians would have seen the hand change.\f*.
\s1 In My Own Hand
\p
\v 22 If anyone does not love the Lord, let him be under the ban\f + \fr 16:22 \ft Greek \tl anathema\tl*, from Hebrew \tl cherem\tl* — devoted to destruction, put outside (Leviticus 27:28-29). At 12:3 it was what no one in the Spirit says of Jesus; here it falls on the man who does not love him. Love for the Lord is the last boundary of this community, and everything else in the letter has been a failure of it.\f*. Marana tha\f + \fr 16:22 \ft Aramaic, kept untranslated: \tl marana tha\tl*, "our Lord, come!" The oldest Aramaic-speaking believers cried it in worship and the Greek-speaking assemblies kept the words as they stood. The Didache places the same cry immediately after a warning to the unholy, exactly as here.\f*!
\v 23 The favor of the Lord Jesus be with you.
\v 24 My love be with you all in Christ Jesus \add [— all of you, whichever party you have been claiming]\add*.
